<section class="bg-cream">
  <div class="container content">
    <h2>Nevada Mining Questions</h2>
    <h3>Do I need a water source to run a wash plant in Nevada?</h3>
    <p>No. Most Nevada placer ground is far from a reliable creek, so we configure Nevada wash plants for closed-loop recirculation off a settling pond. A single water haul can keep a plant running for days of production.</p>
    <h3>What size gold wash plant is right for a Nevada claim?</h3>
    <p>For testing a new claim, a 50-ton plant is the usual starting point. Established commercial operations in districts like Round Mountain or Battle Mountain typically run 100 to 200 ton plants. We size the plant to your yardage and water budget.</p>
    <h3>Can you ship a wash plant to a remote Nevada claim?</h3>
    <p>Yes. We coordinate freight to the nearest road access for claims on BLM ground across the Great Basin, and our plants set up in about 30 minutes once on site.</p>
    <h3>How does Nevada's dry, cemented ground affect gold recovery?</h3>
    <p>Caliche-cemented desert gravels trap gold until they are broken apart. Our wash plants use aggressive scrubbing to free that gold before it reaches the sluice, which is critical for recovery on Nevada placer ground.</p>
  </div>
</section>

